  "explanation": {
    "short": "In German, ei sounds like English \"eye\" (heiße), au like \"ow\" in \"how\" (Haus), eu like \"oy\" (freut), sch like \"sh\" (Schwester) and w like English \"v\" (Wasser). ß is a sharp s. Every noun starts with a capital letter: Schwester, Wasser.",
    "more": "ie is the opposite of ei: it is a long \"ee\" (vier). German spelling is fairly regular, and a few more patterns (long and short vowels, s, j, word endings) come in later steps. It is a guide, not a promise: some words are exceptions."
  },
